<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Cities of Menton and Roquebrune</h1><p class="st-meta">1848 CE – 1861 CE · capital Menton</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Two Riviera towns that broke away from Monaco in 1848 over taxes on their lemons and lived as free cities until France bought them.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roquebrune-cap-martin">Roquebrune</a> formed most of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco</a>'s territory and income, through their lemon and olive trade. Resentment at Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florestan-i-prince-of-monaco">Florestan I</a>'s taxes and monopolies boiled over in 1848, and in March the two towns declared themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> under the protection of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The towns governed themselves through elected councils, with Sardinian troops present. A plebiscite favoured union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, but it was never formally completed because of diplomatic objections. Their economy remained based on citrus fruit, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> began to attract winter visitors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1860, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roquebrune-cap-martin">Roquebrune</a> voted to join France. In 1861 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-prince-of-monaco">Charles III</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-monaco">Monaco</a> sold his rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-french-empire">Second French Empire</a> for four million francs.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-cities-of-menton-and-roquebrune.jpg" alt="Free Cities of Menton and Roquebrune" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_Cities_of_Menton_and_Roquebrune_(1848-1849).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1848 CE – 1861 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Menton</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan and Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free City of Menton, Menton and Roquebrune</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=free-cities-of-menton-and-roquebrune" aria-label="Free Cities of Menton and Roquebrune on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4860 627 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Cities of Menton and Roquebrune lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5180 883L5178 884M5180 883L5226 1028M5180 883L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5178 884h0M5226 1028h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5180 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5180 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Menton. <a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=free-cities-of-menton-and-roquebrune">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
